## Path A — AWS EC2 Image Builder (Recommended)

Image Builder runs your component inside a managed pipeline and handles AMI hardening, tagging, and distribution. The two artifacts (`component.yaml` + `image-builder-recipe.json`) compose into one pipeline.
{{- if eq .Install "native" }}

### Prerequisites (native install only)

The `component.yaml` installs Horde by syncing binaries from S3. Before proceeding:

1. Publish the Horde server binaries from your UE source checkout:
   ```bash
   cd Engine/Source/Programs/Horde
   dotnet publish HordeServer/HordeServer.csproj -c Release -o /tmp/horde-bin
   ```
2. Upload them to an S3 bucket your Image Builder instance profile can read:
   ```bash
   aws s3 sync /tmp/horde-bin/ s3://YOUR_BUCKET/horde-bin/
   ```
3. Open `component.yaml` and replace `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_BUCKET` with your actual bucket name.
{{- else }}

### Prerequisites (docker install)

`ghcr.io/epicgames/horde-server` is **private** — the operator-side pull needs a
GitHub token with `read:packages` scope. The bake pulls the server image from an
**ECR repository** using the build instance role (no secrets baked into the AMI),
so mirror the image into ECR first:

1. Push the image to an ECR repository your Image Builder instance profile can
   read (create the repository if needed):
   ```bash
   ECR_REPO=<account>.dkr.ecr.{{ .Region }}.amazonaws.com/horde-server
   # Operator host: pull from GHCR with a read:packages token, then push to ECR
   echo "<YOUR_GITHUB_PAT>" | docker login ghcr.io -u <YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME> --password-stdin
   docker pull ghcr.io/epicgames/horde-server:{{ .Version }}
   docker tag  ghcr.io/epicgames/horde-server:{{ .Version }} "${ECR_REPO}:{{ .Version }}"
   aws ecr get-login-password --region {{ .Region }} | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <account>.dkr.ecr.{{ .Region }}.amazonaws.com
   docker push "${ECR_REPO}:{{ .Version }}"
   ```
2. Open `component.yaml` and replace `REPLACE_WITH_ECR_REPOSITORY` with the
   repository URL (e.g. `<account>.dkr.ecr.{{ .Region }}.amazonaws.com/horde-server`).

**IAM for the Image Builder instance profile** — the bake calls
`ecr get-authorization-token` and pulls the image:

| Action | Resource |
|--------|----------|
| `ecr:GetAuthorizationToken` | `*` (**required** — a repo-scoped ARN returns AccessDenied) |
| `ecr:BatchGetImage` | the repository ARN (repo-scoped is fine) |
| `ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer` | the repository ARN (repo-scoped is fine) |

The auth-token call cannot be scoped to a repository ARN, so `GetAuthorizationToken`
must stay on `Resource: "*"`.

The bake also installs the AWS CLI v2 (`/usr/local/bin/aws`). If you run any of the
verification below over SSM `AWS-RunShellScript`, call it by full path —
`/usr/local/bin` is not on the SSM session `PATH`.
{{- end }}

### 1. Create the custom component

```bash
aws imagebuilder create-component \
  --region {{ .Region }} \
  --name {{ .Name }}-{{ .Install }} \
  --semantic-version 1.0.0 \
  --platform Linux \
  --supported-os-versions "Ubuntu 22" \
  --data file://component.yaml
```

The command prints the new component's ARN — copy it. It looks like:

```
arn:aws:imagebuilder:{{ .Region }}:000000000000:component/{{ .Name }}-{{ .Install }}/1.0.0/1
```

### 2. Substitute the ARN into the recipe

`image-builder-recipe.json` contains a placeholder string `REPLACE_WITH_CUSTOM_COMPONENT_ARN`. Replace it with the ARN from step 1.

### 3. Create the recipe

```bash
aws imagebuilder create-image-recipe \
  --region {{ .Region }} \
  --cli-input-json file://image-builder-recipe.json
```

### 4. Create an infrastructure configuration and image pipeline

Use the AWS console (Image Builder → Image pipelines → Create) or `aws imagebuilder create-infrastructure-configuration` / `create-image-pipeline`. This part is one-time setup per AWS account.

### 5. Run the pipeline

```bash
aws imagebuilder start-image-pipeline-execution \
  --region {{ .Region }} \
  --image-pipeline-arn <pipeline-arn>
```

When the pipeline finishes, note the new AMI ID and update `fabrica.yaml`:

```yaml
horde:
  amiId: ami-<id>
```

